---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy - Vesper"
  description="What Vesper and Vesper Kids collect, why, where it is stored, how long it is kept and how to delete it."
  current="privacy"
>
  <section class="wrap policy-head" aria-labelledby="policy-title">
    <h1 id="policy-title">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Vesper and Vesper Kids. Last updated: 8 October 2026.</p>
    <ul class="short" aria-label="The short version">
      <li>No ads, no analytics, no advertising ID.</li>
      <li>Without an account, your blocks and screen time stay on your phone.</li>
      <li>A linked child's phone shares its usage only with the parent's account.</li>
      <li>Delete your account, and everything stored with it goes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="doc">
    <nav class="doc-toc" aria-label="Sections">
      <ol>
          <li><a href="#who-we-are">Who we are</a></li>
          <li><a href="#without-an-account">Without an account</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-we-collect-if-you-sign-in-or-link-a-phone">What we collect if you sign in or link a phone</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ai-block-builder">AI block builder</a></li>
          <li><a href="#permissions">Permissions</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-we-use-it">Why we use it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-it-is-stored-and-who-processes-it">Where it is stored and who processes it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-long-we-keep-it">How long we keep it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#deleting-your-data">Deleting your data</a></li>
          <li><a href="#children">Children</a></li>
          <li><a href="#contact">Contact</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <article class="doc-body">
    <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are</h2>
    <p>Vesper and Vesper Kids are free, open-source (GPL-3.0) Android apps. The source code is at <a href="https://github.com/dculussoftwares/vesper">github.com/dculussoftwares/vesper</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="without-an-account">Without an account</h2>
    <p>You can use the app without signing in. Your blocked apps, schedules, limits and usage statistics stay on your
      device. We do not receive them. The only exception is the optional AI feature described below.</p>

    <h2 id="what-we-collect-if-you-sign-in-or-link-a-phone">What we collect if you sign in or link a phone</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Account:</strong> your email address, display name and a unique account ID (Firebase
        Authentication). If you use Google sign-in, Google gives us your email and name; we do not receive your
        Google password.</li>
      <li><strong>Child phone status:</strong> the phone's name and model, whether it is online, when it was last
        seen, its battery level and whether it is charging, and the app that is currently in the foreground (Cloud
        Firestore).</li>
      <li><strong>Child phone app activity:</strong> the list of launchable apps installed on the child phone (package
        name and label), and daily usage per app (minutes and number of opens), so a parent can see screen time and
        choose apps to block (Cloud Firestore).</li>
      <li><strong>Rules and messages:</strong> the blocks, limits and schedules a parent sets, commands sent to the
        child phone, and the requests (for example "more time") and alerts (for example a revoked permission)
        exchanged between the two phones (Cloud Firestore).</li>
      <li><strong>Notification token:</strong> a push token for each parent phone, used to deliver notifications
        (Firebase Cloud Messaging).</li>
      <li><strong>Link codes:</strong> a short-lived code and the child phone's model, used once to link the phones.
        They are valid for five minutes and are then deleted automatically; deletion can take up to a day after expiry.</li>
      <li><strong>Firebase SDK data:</strong> the Firebase services above process technical data needed to operate
        and secure them, such as app instance and installation identifiers, IP address, and device and app
        attestation signals (Firebase App Check with Play Integrity). We do not use Firebase Analytics or Crashlytics
        and do not collect advertising IDs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="ai-block-builder">AI block builder</h2>
    <p>If you use the option to describe a block in a sentence, such as "block Instagram after 9 pm", that sentence is
      sent to Google's Gemini model through Firebase AI Logic, and the reply is used to fill in the block form. Do not
      put personal details in the sentence. If the AI is unavailable, the app interprets the sentence on your device
      instead. We do not keep the sentence unless you choose "Report a bad result": after you confirm what will be sent, that
      sentence and the draft the AI produced are stored with your account so we can improve the feature. Google
      processes the sentence under its Firebase and Gemini API terms.</p>

    <h2 id="permissions">Permissions</h2>
    <p>The app uses Usage Access and an Accessibility service only to detect which app is in the foreground so it can
      block it and measure screen time. On a phone without an account this stays on the device. On a child phone that
      a parent has linked, the current app and daily usage are sent to the linked parent as described above. We do not
      read the content of any app, keystrokes, messages or the screen.</p>

    <h2 id="why-we-use-it">Why we use it</h2>
    <p>Only to run the features above: signing you in, linking a parent and a child phone, showing screen time,
      enforcing blocks and delivering requests and alerts. No profiling and no ads.</p>

    <h2 id="where-it-is-stored-and-who-processes-it">Where it is stored and who processes it</h2>
    <p>Account and device data is stored in Google Cloud Firestore and Firebase Authentication, and is encrypted in
      transit (HTTPS/TLS) and at rest by Google. Access rules limit each account's data to its owner and the phones
      linked to it. Google Firebase processes it on our behalf. We do not sell it, do not share it with anyone else and
      do not show ads.</p>

    <h2 id="how-long-we-keep-it">How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>Account and device data is kept until you delete it: unlinking a phone deletes that phone's data, and deleting
      your account deletes everything stored with it. Link codes stop working after five minutes and are deleted automatically, which can take up to a day. Firebase
      may keep short-lived technical logs and backups for a limited period before they are overwritten.</p>

    <h2 id="deleting-your-data">Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>You can delete your account and all data stored with it in the app (Profile, Delete account and all data) or on
      the <a href="/delete-account">account deletion page</a>. You can also unlink a single phone from the app.
      Data kept only on your phone is removed by uninstalling the app.</p>

    <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
    <p>Vesper Kids is installed by a parent or guardian on their child's phone and is controlled by that parent.
      Children do not create accounts or enter personal details. The data collected from the child phone is available
      only to the linked parent account and is deleted when the parent unlinks the phone or deletes the account.</p>

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <p>Open an issue at <a href="https://github.com/dculussoftwares/vesper/issues">github.com/dculussoftwares/vesper/issues</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .policy-head {
    padding-top: clamp(48px, 7vw, 120px);
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
  }

  .policy-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--t-display);
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  .short {
    margin: clamp(48px, 6vw, 96px) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 40px 24px;
    font-size: var(--t-statement);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .short li {
    max-width: 24ch;
    padding-top: 20px;
    border-top: 4px solid var(--green);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .short {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Mark the section in view in the index.
  const links = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>(".doc-toc a")];
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      for (const entry of entries) {
        if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
        links.forEach((a) => a.classList.toggle("active", a.hash === `#${entry.target.id}`));
      }
    },
    { rootMargin: "0px 0px -70% 0px" },
  );
  document.querySelectorAll(".doc-body h2[id]").forEach((h) => observer.observe(h));
</script>
